A||||5||fieroman_5000||How Do I fix those rubbing door/fender body panels??||||1||
Z||000000||fieroman_5000||12-18-2001||08:06 AM||||Well, how do I? Is there a way of adjusting the position of the body panels? (Door and fender to be precise) If there is how do I do it?<P>Fieroman <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indyrestoguy_JR||12-18-2001||08:11 AM||||That would depend on the problem.<BR>By the way, what IS the problem?<P>IRG-not JR||||reg||1
Z||000002||fieroman_5000||12-18-2001||04:51 PM||||Well...think about it like this, the crack where the door panel and the fender panel meet rub...right on the edges of the body panels.<P>Fieroman <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||12-18-2001||07:13 PM||||are they both even at the crack (flat)? the fenders and door panels are all on with screws and bolts thru slotted holes so you can loosen them and move it around. adjust the lower door hinge bolts if the door is sticking out past the fender slightly, they should be even. If there just too close together, you can loosen the bolts along the top edge of fender and slide it forward. A little bit goes a long ways. You may need a small prybar to hold it apart a little while you tighten them. Put some small scraps of wood between so you dont crack the paint.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 12-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||12-18-2001||07:26 PM||||try this....<P>Loosen all the screws for the door skin. Don't forget the ones under the trim. <P>Don't worry about the bottom rivits... They'll slide. The mirror should be the same way.<P><u>Remove the screw by the out side lock.</u> This one screw is not slotted. (and yes for a reason.) Note that behind this screw is an adjustable plastic plug. (You can see that better with the inside skin off.)<P>Carefully shift the panel front or back as needed. The skin should float in a range about 1/4-3/8 inch as I remember.<P>If you are very close to the old hole in the plug by the lock, you have 2 choices. Rotate the plug slightly or stuff the hole. If you stuff the olg hole, glue the stuffing in place. (Plastic Weld epoxy is best but anything will likely do.)<P>GM just drives the screw into that block to lock the skin location. You can do the same or drill a small pilot hole. Use a small drill.<P>Note that this could expose bare areas under the mirror trim. A slight tweek of the mirror may or may not cover it agian. depends how far you moved the skin.<P>This little bit of missing info is part of what has led many people to think doors can't be moved from one car to another.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||fieroman_5000||12-20-2001||05:52 PM||||Thanx Ogre U R tha Man!!! It worked...just needed a little shifting of the panels.     <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.btinternet.co.uk/sm/notworthy.gif"> <P>Fieroman <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
